1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:06,400
when they ripened, should be reserved for pigs alone.

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,199
Chapter 4.

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:14,079
By the late summer, the news of what had happened on Animal Farm had spread

4
00:00:14,079 --> 00:00:16,159
across half the county.

5
00:00:16,159 --> 00:00:20,760
Every day, Snowball and Napoleon sent out flights of pigeons, whose

6
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:25,079
instructions were to mingle with the animals on neighboring farms, tell them

7
00:00:25,079 --> 00:00:30,920
the story of the rebellion, and teach them the tune of Beasts of England.

8
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:34,960
Most of this time, Mr. Jones had spent sitting in the tap room of the Red Lion

9
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:39,639
at Willingdon, complaining to anyone who would listen of the monstrous injustice

10
00:00:39,639 --> 00:00:45,440
he had suffered in being turned out of his property by a pack of good-for-nothing animals.

11
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:51,519
The other farmers sympathized in principle, but they did not at first give him much help.

12
00:00:51,519 --> 00:00:55,559
At heart, each of them was secretly wondering whether he could not somehow

13
00:00:55,559 --> 00:01:00,719
turn Jones's misfortune to his own advantage.

14
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:05,480
It was lucky that the owners of the two farms which adjoined Animal Farm were on

15
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,000
permanently bad terms.

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,400
One of them, which was named Foxwood, was a large, neglected, old-fashioned

17
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:18,519
farm, much overgrown by woodland, with all its pastures worn out, and its

18
00:01:18,519 --> 00:01:21,599
hedges in a disgraceful condition.

19
00:01:21,599 --> 00:01:26,519
Its owner, Mr. Pilkington, was an easy-going gentleman farmer who spent

20
00:01:26,519 --> 00:01:31,400
most of his time in fishing or hunting, according to the season.

21
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:36,800
The other farm, which was called Pinchfield, was smaller and better kept.

22
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:42,360
Its owner was a Mr. Frederick, a tough, shrewd man, perpetually involved in

23
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:46,880
lawsuits, and with a name for driving hard bargains.

24
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,960
These two disliked each other so much that it was difficult for them to come

25
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:55,320
to any agreement, even in defense of their own interests.

26
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:59,559
Nevertheless, they were both thoroughly frightened by the rebellion on Animal

27
00:01:59,559 --> 00:02:06,000
Farm, and very anxious to prevent their own animals from learning too much about it.

28
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
At first, they pretended to laugh, to scorn the idea of animals managing a farm for themselves.

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,119
The whole thing would be over in a fortnight, they said.

30
00:02:16,119 --> 00:02:20,840
They put it about that the animals on the Manor Farm, they insisted on calling

31
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:27,880
it the Manor Farm, they would not tolerate the name Animal Farm, were perpetually fighting among

32
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:32,960
themselves, and were also rapidly starving to death.

33
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:38,119
When time passed, and the animals had evidently not starved to death,

34
00:02:38,119 --> 00:02:43,000
Frederick and Pilkington changed their tune, and began to talk of the terrible

35
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,360
wickedness that now flourished on Animal Farm.

36
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:52,000
It was given out that the animals there practiced cannibalism, tortured one

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,800
another with red-hot horseshoes, and had their females in common.

38
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:04,000
This was what came of rebelling against the laws of nature, Frederick and Pilkington said.

39
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,320
However, these stories were never fully believed.

40
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:13,000
Rumors of a wonderful farm where the human beings had been turned out, and

41
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,280
the animals managed their own affairs, continued to circulate in vague and distorted forms.

42
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:26,480
And throughout that year, a wave of rebelliousness ran through the countryside.

43
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:32,440
Bulls, which had always been tractable, suddenly turned savage. Sheep broke down

44
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:35,199
hedges and devoured the clover.

45
00:03:35,199 --> 00:03:40,440
Cows kicked the pail over. Hunters refused their fences and shot their

46
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:43,199
riders onto the other side.

47
00:03:43,199 --> 00:03:51,679
Above all, the tune and even the words of Beasts of England were known everywhere.

48
00:03:51,679 --> 00:03:55,079
It had spread with astonishing speed.

49
00:03:55,079 --> 00:03:59,639
The human beings could not contain their rage when they heard this song, though

50
00:03:59,639 --> 00:04:03,199
they pretended to think it merely ridiculous.

51
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:08,079
They could not understand, they said, how even animals could bring themselves

52
00:04:08,079 --> 00:04:11,639
to sing such contemptible rubbish.

53
00:04:11,639 --> 00:04:16,760
Any animal caught singing it was given a flogging on the spot.

54
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:20,800
And yet, the song was irrepressible.

55
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:26,559
The blackbirds whistled it in the hedges, the pigeons cooed it in the elms.

56
00:04:26,559 --> 00:04:31,760
It got into the din of the smithies and the tune of the church bells.

57
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:36,880
And when the human beings listened to it, they secretly trembled, hearing in

58
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:40,760
it a prophecy of their future doom.

59
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:45,280
Early in October, when the corn was cut and stacked and some of it was already

60
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:50,159
threshed, a flight of pigeons came whirling through the air and alighted in

61
00:04:50,159 --> 00:04:54,679
the yard of Animal Farm in the wildest excitement.

62
00:04:54,679 --> 00:05:00,159
Jones and all his men, with half a dozen others from Foxwood and Pinchfield, had

63
00:05:00,159 --> 00:05:06,079
entered the five-barred gate and were coming up the cart track that led to the farm.

64
00:05:06,079 --> 00:05:13,440
They were all carrying sticks, except Jones, who was marching ahead with a gun in his hands.

65
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:18,719
Obviously, they were going to attempt the recapture of the farm.

66
00:05:18,719 --> 00:05:23,920
This had long been expected, and all preparations had been made.

67
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:28,760
Snowball, who had studied an old book of Julius Caesar's campaigns, which he had

68
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:33,639
found in the farmhouse, was in charge of the defensive operations.

69
